Here's the Braze Liquid for your cart block:

```liquid
{% comment %} Get cart size — filters only work in assign, not in if {% endcomment %}
{% assign cart_size = {{custom_attribute.${cart_items}}} | size %}

{% if cart_size > 3 %}
  <div style="padding: 20px; background: #f5f5f5; margin: 20px 0;">
    <h2>Your cart is filling up!</h2>

    {% for item in {{custom_attribute.${cart_items}}} limit: 3 %}
      <div style="margin: 10px 0;">
        {{item.name | escape}} — {{item.price | money}}
      </div>
    {% endfor %}

    {% comment %} Two custom attributes — assign one first, then add them {% endcomment %}
    {% assign rewards = {{custom_attribute.${rewards_balance}}} | default: 0 %}
    {% assign total_balance = {{custom_attribute.${giftcard_balance}}} | default: 0 | plus: {{rewards}} %}
    <p>Your total balance: {{total_balance | money}}</p>
  </div>
{% endif %}
```

**Key points:**

- This assumes `cart_items`, `rewards_balance`, and `giftcard_balance` are all **custom attributes**. If any are standard attributes, the syntax changes.
- The `| size` filter must be in an `{% assign %}` — Braze doesn't allow filters inside `{% if %}`.
- You can't reference two custom attributes in one expression, so `rewards` is assigned first before adding to `giftcard_balance`.
- The `| money` filter assumes your values are already in dollars. If they're stored in cents, pipe through `| divided_by: 100.00` first.

**Test with:**
- A user with exactly 3 items (block should NOT show)
- A user with 4+ items (block should show, with only first 3)
- A user with missing `rewards_balance` or `giftcard_balance` (defaults to 0)
- A user with no `cart_items` at all (block won't show, no error)

