baseline.response:
Here's how to set up your Braze email to abort if the API doesn't return at least 3 products:

```liquid
{% connected_content https://your-api.example.com/recommendations :save recs %}

{% comment %} Check for API failure or insufficient products {% endcomment %}
{% if recs.__http_status_code__ != 200 or recs.products.size < 3 %}
  {% abort_message('product recommendations unavailable') %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %} Now safe to render the 3-across row {% endcomment %}
<tr>
  <td>{{recs.products[0].name}}</td>
  <td>{{recs.products[1].name}}</td>
  <td>{{recs.products[2].name}}</td>
</tr>
```

**Key points:**

1. **`__http_status_code__`** — When the API returns non-200 or times out (>2 seconds), Braze renders the response as **empty** rather than erroring. You must explicitly check the status code.

2. **Size check** — Use `recs.products.size < 3` (adjust `products` to match your actual array name in the API response). Remember: you can't use filters inside `{% if %}`, so if you need to do something like `| size`, assign it first:
   ```liquid
   {% assign product_count = recs.products | size %}
   {% if recs.__http_status_code__ != 200 or product_count < 3 %}
   ```

3. **`abort_message`** — The reason string **must be a static string in quotes**. Liquid variables inside it don't work.

4. **Abort behavior** — An aborted message doesn't send, doesn't appear on the user profile, doesn't count toward deliveries, and doesn't count toward frequency capping.

5. **`:retry`** — If you add `:retry` to the Connected Content call, it will retry up to 5 times with backoff, but **abort logic takes precedence**. If the same condition triggers both, abort wins and retries never run.

**To test:** Use **Preview & Test → Preview as Custom User** in Braze. Note that `:retry` doesn't run in previews, so you'll only see the abort behavior when testing live sends.

